<h2>Explain SQL Queries</h2>
<p>We next need to discuss how the BusTub shell =E2=80=9Cknows=E2=80=9D tha=
t <code>SELECT * FROM __mock_table_1;</code> should read everything from <c=
ode>__mock_table_1</code> when given that input. BusTub also supports the <=
code>EXPLAIN</code> command for printing a query's execution plan. You can =
add <code>EXPLAIN</code> in front of any query to see this information:</p>
<pre>bustub&gt; EXPLAIN SELECT * FROM __mock_table_1;
=3D=3D=3D BINDER =3D=3D=3D
BoundSelect {
  table=3DBoundBaseTableRef { table=3D__mock_table_1, oid=3D0 },
  columns=3D[__mock_table_1.colA, __mock_table_1.colB],
  groupBy=3D[],
  having=3D,
  where=3D,
  limit=3D,
  offset=3D,
  order_by=3D[],
  is_distinct=3Dfalse,
}
=3D=3D=3D PLANNER =3D=3D=3D
Projection { exprs=3D[#0.0, #0.1] } | (__mock_table_1.colA:INTEGER, __mock_=
table_1.colB:INTEGER)
MockScan { table=3D__mock_table_1 } | (__mock_table_1.colA:INTEGER, __mock_=
table_1.colB:INTEGER)
=3D=3D=3D OPTIMIZER =3D=3D=3D
MockScan { table=3D__mock_table_1 } | (__mock_table_1.colA:INTEGER, __mock_=
table_1.colB:INTEGER)
</pre>

<p>The result of <code>EXPLAIN</code> provides an overview of the transform=
ation process within the query processing layer. The statement first goes i=
nto the parser and the binder, which produces an AST (abstract syntax tree)=
. This is how BusTub understands what the query wants to do. In this exampl=
e, the query wants to perform a <code>BoundSelect</code> on <code>__mock_ta=
ble_1</code> and retrieve two columns (i.e., <code>colA</code> and <code>co=
lB</code>). Note that the binder automatically expands the <code>*</code> c=
haracter in the original SQL query into the actual columns in the table.</p=
>
<p>Next, the binder AST goes into the planner, and the planner will produce=
 a query plan corresponding to the query. The query is planned as a tree of=
 two nodes. The data flows from the leaves of the tree to the root.</p>
<p><img src=3D"https://15445.courses.cs.cmu.edu/fall2022/project3/img/mock_=
scan.svg" width=3D"100%" style=3D"border: 1px solid gray;"></p>
<p>After that, the optimizer will optimize the query plan. In this case, it=
 removes the projection plan node because it is redundant.</p>
<p>Let us consider a more complex example:</p>
<pre>bustub&gt; EXPLAIN (o) SELECT colA, MAX(colB) FROM
  (SELECT * FROM __mock_table_1, __mock_table_3 WHERE colA =3D colE) GROUP =
BY colA;
=3D=3D=3D OPTIMIZER =3D=3D=3D
Agg { types=3D[max], aggregates=3D[#0.1], group_by=3D[#0.0] }
  NestedLoopJoin { type=3DInner, predicate=3D(#0.0=3D#1.0) }
    MockScan { table=3D__mock_table_1 }
    MockScan { table=3D__mock_table_3 }
</pre>

<p>In BusTub, the optimizer output is always a tree. For this example query=
, the tree is as follows:</p>
<p><img src=3D"https://15445.courses.cs.cmu.edu/fall2022/project3/img/more_=
complex_example.svg" width=3D"100%" style=3D"border: 1px solid gray;"></p>
<p>In this project, you will need to construct SQL queries to test each of =
your executor's implementations. <code>EXPLAIN</code> is extremely importan=
t for you to know if a SQL query is using a specific executor.</p>
<p class=3D"warning bg-info">
  <i class=3D"trigger fa fa-question-circle fa-lg" data-eusoft-scrollable-e=
lement=3D"1"></i>
  If you are running this example in the web shell, you might see <code>Has=
hJoin</code> instead of <code>NestedLoopJoin</code>. You do <u>not</u> need=
 to implement <code>HashJoin</code> this semester.
</p>
<!-- **********************************************************************=
 -->
<!-- Samples                                                               =
 -->
<!-- **********************************************************************=
 -->
<h2>Sample Executors</h2>
<p>In the BusTub public repository, we already provide the implementation o=
f several executors. Let's see when the planner will plan SQL queries as th=
ese plan nodes.</p>
<!-- ::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::: -->
<!-- Projection                                -->
<!-- ::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::: -->
<div class=3D"code-class">
<h3>Projection</h3>
<p>A <strong>projection</strong> plan node is used to do computation over a=
n input. It will always have exactly one child. Try running the following e=
xample queries in the BusTub shell and examine their output:</p>
<pre>EXPLAIN SELECT 1 + 2;
EXPLAIN SELECT colA FROM __mock_table_1;
EXPLAIN SELECT colA + colB AS a, 1 + 2 AS b FROM __mock_table_1;
</pre>
<p>A projection plan node contains several expressions for computation. It =
can be <code>ColumnValueExpression</code> (displayed as <code>#0.0</code> w=
hen explained), which indicates directly placing a column of the child exec=
utor to this output column; or a <code>ConstantExpression</code>, which is =
a constant value (e.g., <code>1</code>). Expressions are also represented a=
s a tree. For example, <code>1 + 2</code> is an <code>ArithmeticExpression<=
/code> with two <code>ConstantExpression</code> (<code>1</code> and <code>2=
</code>) as children. </p>
<p>Please note that the syntax <code>#0.0</code> means that the first colum=
n in the first child. You will see something like <code>#0.0 =3D #1.0</code=
> when planning joins.</p>
</div>
<!-- ::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::: -->
<!-- Filter                                    -->
<!-- ::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::: -->
<div class=3D"code-class">
<h3>Filter</h3>
<p>A <strong>filter</strong> plan node is used to filter the output of a ch=
ild using a given predicate. For example,</p>
<pre>EXPLAIN SELECT * FROM __mock_table_1 WHERE colA &gt; 1;
</pre>
<p>A filter plan node has exactly one child and contains a predicate.</p>
</div>
<!-- ::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::: -->
<!-- Values                                    -->
<!-- ::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::: -->
<div class=3D"code-class">
<h3>Values</h3>
<p>A <strong>values</strong> plan node is used to directly produce values.<=
/p>
<pre>EXPLAIN values (1, 2, 'a'), (3, 4, 'b');
CREATE TABLE table1(v1 INT, v2 INT, v3 VARCHAR(128));
EXPLAIN INSERT INTO table1 VALUES (1, 2, 'a'), (3, 4, 'b');
</pre>
<p>Values plan nodes are useful when inserting into a table with values sup=
plied by the user.</p>
</div>
<h2>Schema</h2>
<p>As you might have noticed, when explaining, there is a long string of co=
lumn descriptions after each plan node. It's the expected <strong>output sc=
hema</strong> of this plan node. Consider this example:</p>
<pre>Projection { exprs=3D[#0.0, #0.1] } | (__mock_table_1.colA:INTEGER, __=
mock_table_1.colB:INTEGER)
</pre>

<p>This indicates that the executor representing this plan node will produc=
e two columns. Both of them are of integer types. The output schema is infe=
rred within the planner. For this project, your executor implementations mu=
st produce tuples with schema exactly as specified in the plan node, otherw=
ise it will fail tests that check the output.</p>
