=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=
=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=
 -->
<!-- PROJECT SPECIFICATION                                                 =
    -->
<!-- =3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=
=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=
=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=
 -->
<h1 data-eusoft-scrollable-element=3D"1">Project Specification</h1>
<p>For this project, you will need to implement additional operator executo=
rs in BusTub. We will use the <a href=3D"https://15445.courses.cs.cmu.edu/f=
all2022/slides/12-queryexecution1.pdf">iterator</a> query processing model =
(i.e., the Volcano model). Recall that in this model, every query plan exec=
utor implements a Next function. When the DBMS invokes an executor's Next f=
unction, the executor returns either (1) a single tuple or (2) an indicator=
 that there are no more tuples. With this approach, each executor implement=
s a loop that continues calling Next on its children to retrieve tuples and=
 process them one-by-one.</p>
<p>In BusTub's implementation of the iterator model, the <code>Next</code> =
function for each executor returns a record identifier (<code>RID</code>) i=
n addition to a tuple. A record identifier serves as a unique identifier fo=
r the tuple.</p>
<p>The executors are created from an execution plan in <a href=3D"https://g=
ithub.com/cmu-db/bustub/blob/master/src/execution/executor_factory.cpp"><tt=
 class=3D"filepath">executor_factory.cpp</tt></a>.</p>
<p>All the test cases in this project are written in a special file format =
called SQLLogicTest (derived from SQLite). You can find how to use it at th=
e end of this page.</p>
<!-- **********************************************************************=
 -->
<!-- Task #1 - Access Method Executors                                     =
-->
<!-- **********************************************************************=
 -->
<h2 id=3D"task1">Task #1 - Access Method Executors</h2>
<p>In the background section, we saw that the BusTub is already able to ret=
rieve data from mock tables in <code>SELECT</code> queries. This is because=
 these are special tables that do not actually store real tuples. Instead t=
hey are "virtual" tables that use the <code>MockScan</code> executor to alw=
ays generate the same tuples using a predefined algorithm. This is why you =
cannot update these tables.</p>
<p>In this task, you will need to implement executors that read from and wr=
ite to the tables in the storage. You will complete your implementation in =
the following files:</p>
<ul>
<li><tt class=3D"filepath"> src/include/execution/seq_scan_executor.h</tt><=
/li>
<li><tt class=3D"filepath"> src/execution/seq_scan_executor.cpp</tt></li>
<li><tt class=3D"filepath"> src/include/execution/insert_executor.h</tt></l=
i>
<li><tt class=3D"filepath"> src/execution/insert_executor.cpp</tt></li>
<li><tt class=3D"filepath"> src/include/execution/delete_executor.h</tt></l=
i>
<li><tt class=3D"filepath"> src/execution/delete_executor.cpp</tt></li>
<li><tt class=3D"filepath"> src/include/execution/index_scan_executor.h</tt=
></li>
<li><tt class=3D"filepath"> src/execution/index_scan_executor.cpp</tt></li>
</ul>
<!-- ::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::: -->
<!-- SeqScan                                   -->
<!-- ::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::: -->
<div class=3D"code-class">
<h3>SeqScan</h3>
<p>The <a href=3D"https://github.com/cmu-db/bustub/blob/master/src/include/=
execution/plans/seq_scan_plan.h"><code>SeqScanPlanNode</code></a> can be pl=
anned with a <code>SELECT * from table</code> statement.</p>
<pre>bustub&gt; CREATE TABLE t1(v1 INT, v2 VARCHAR(100));
Table created with id =3D 15
bustub&gt; EXPLAIN (o,s) SELECT * FROM t1;
=3D=3D=3D OPTIMIZER =3D=3D=3D
SeqScan { table=3Dt1 } | (t1.v1:INTEGER, t1.v2:VARCHAR)
</pre>
<p>The <code>SeqScanExecutor</code> iterates over a table and returns its t=
uples, one-at-a-time.</p>
<p><strong>Hint:</strong> Be careful when using the <code>TableIterator</co=
de> object. Make sure that you understand the difference between the pre-in=
crement and post-increment operators. You may find yourself getting strange=
 output by switching between <code>++iter</code> and <code>iter++</code>.</=
p>
<p><strong>Hint:</strong> The output of sequential scan is a copy of each m=
atched tuple and its original record identifier (<code>RID</code>).</p>
<!-- **Hint:** We have some sample tables with pre-populated data. You can =
issue `SELECT` on those tables. -->
<p><strong>Note:</strong> BusTub does not support <code>DROP TABLE</code> o=
r <code>DROP INDEX</code> for now. You can reset your database by simply re=
starting the shell.</p>
</div>
<!-- ::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::: -->
<!-- Insert                                    -->
<!-- ::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::: -->
<div class=3D"code-class">
<h3>Insert</h3>
<p>The <a href=3D"https://github.com/cmu-db/bustub/blob/master/src/include/=
execution/plans/insert_plan.h"><code>InsertPlanNode</code></a> can be plann=
ed with a <code>INSERT</code> statement. Note that you will need to use a s=
ingle quote to specify a <code>VARCHAR</code> value.</p>
<pre>bustub&gt; EXPLAIN (o,s) INSERT INTO t1 VALUES (1, 'a'), (2, 'b');
=3D=3D=3D OPTIMIZER =3D=3D=3D
Insert { table_oid=3D15 } | (__bustub_internal.insert_rows:INTEGER)
  Values { rows=3D2 } | (__values#0.0:INTEGER, __values#0.1:VARCHAR)
</pre>
<p>The <code>InsertExecutor</code> inserts tuples into a table and updates =
indexes. It has exactly one child producing values to be inserted into the =
table. The planner will ensure values have the same schema as the table. Th=
e executor will produce a single tuple of an integer number as the output, =
indicating how many rows have been inserted into the table, after all rows =
are inserted. Remember to update the index when inserting into the table, i=
f it has an index associated with it.</p>
<p><strong>Hint:</strong> You will need to lookup table information for the=
 target of the insert during executor initialization. See the <a href=3D"ht=
tps://15445.courses.cs.cmu.edu/fall2022/project3/#system-catalog">System Ca=
talog</a> section below for additional information on accessing the catalog=
.</p>
<p><strong>Hint:</strong> You will need to update all indexes for the table=
 into which tuples are inserted. See the <a href=3D"https://15445.courses.c=
s.cmu.edu/fall2022/project3/#index-updates">Index Updates</a> section below=
 for further details.</p>
<p><strong>Hint:</strong> You will need to use the <a href=3D"https://githu=
b.com/cmu-db/bustub/blob/master/src/include/storage/table/table_heap.h"><co=
de>TableHeap</code></a> class to perform table modifications.</p>
</div>
<!-- ::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::: -->
<!-- Delete                                    -->
<!-- ::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::: -->
<div class=3D"code-class">
<h3>Delete</h3>
<p>The <a href=3D"https://github.com/cmu-db/bustub/blob/master/src/include/=
execution/plans/delete_plan.h"><code>DeletePlanNode</code></a> can be plann=
ed with a <code>DELETE</code> statement. It has exactly one child with the =
records to be deleted from the table. Your delete executor should produce a=
n integer output that represents the number of rows that it deleted from th=
e table. It will also need to update the index.</p>
<pre>bustub&gt; EXPLAIN (o,s) DELETE FROM t1;
=3D=3D=3D OPTIMIZER =3D=3D=3D
Delete { table_oid=3D15 } | (__bustub_internal.delete_rows:INTEGER)
  Filter { predicate=3Dtrue } | (t1.v1:INTEGER, t1.v2:VARCHAR)
    SeqScan { table=3Dt1 } | (t1.v1:INTEGER, t1.v2:VARCHAR)

bustub&gt; EXPLAIN (o,s) DELETE FROM t1 where v1 =3D 1;
=3D=3D=3D OPTIMIZER =3D=3D=3D
Delete { table_oid=3D15 } | (__bustub_internal.delete_rows:INTEGER)
  Filter { predicate=3D#0.0=3D1 } | (t1.v1:INTEGER, t1.v2:VARCHAR)
    SeqScan { table=3Dt1 } | (t1.v1:INTEGER, t1.v2:VARCHAR)
</pre>
<p>You may assume that the <code>DeleteExecutor</code> is always at the roo=
t of the query plan in which it appears. The <code>DeleteExecutor</code> sh=
ould not modify its result set.</p>
<p><strong>Hint:</strong> You only need to get a <code>RID</code> from the =
child executor and call <a href=3D"https://github.com/cmu-db/bustub/blob/ma=
ster/src/include/storage/table/table_heap.h"><code>TableHeap::MarkDelete()<=
/code></a> to effectively delete the tuple. All deletes will be applied upo=
n transaction commit.</p>
<p><strong>Hint:</strong> You will need to update all indexes for the table=
 from which tuples are deleted. See the <a href=3D"https://15445.courses.cs=
.cmu.edu/fall2022/project3/#index-updates">Index Updates</a> section below =
for further details.</p>
</div>
<!-- ::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::: -->
<!-- IndexScan                                 -->
<!-- ::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::: -->
<div class=3D"code-class">
<h3>IndexScan</h3>
<p>The <code>IndexScanExecutor</code> iterates over an index to retrieve <c=
ode>RIDs</code> for tuples. The operator then uses these RIDs to retrieve t=
heir tuples in the corresponding table. It then emits these tuples one-at-a=
-time. </p>
<p>You can test your index scan executor by <code>SELECT FROM &lt;table&gt;=
 ORDER BY &lt;index column&gt;</code>. We will explain why <code>ORDER BY</=
code> can be transformed into <code>IndexScan</code> in <a href=3D"https://=
15445.courses.cs.cmu.edu/fall2022/project3/#task3">Task #3</a>.</p>
<pre>bustub&gt; CREATE TABLE t2(v3 int, v4 int);
Table created with id =3D 16

bustub&gt; CREATE INDEX t2v3 ON t2(v3);
Index created with id =3D 0

bustub&gt; EXPLAIN (o,s) SELECT * FROM t2 ORDER BY v3;
=3D=3D=3D OPTIMIZER =3D=3D=3D
IndexScan { index_oid=3D0 } | (t2.v3:INTEGER, t2.v4:INTEGER)
</pre>
<p>The type of the index object in the plan will always be <code>BPlusTreeI=
ndexForOneIntegerColumn</code> in this project. You can safely cast it and =
store it in the executor object:</p>
<pre>tree_ =3D dynamic_cast&lt;BPlusTreeIndexForOneIntegerColumn *&gt;(inde=
x_info_-&gt;index_.get())
</pre>
<p>You can then construct index iterator from the index object, scan throug=
h all the keys and tuple IDs, lookup the tuple from the table heap, and emi=
t all tuples in the index key's order as the output of the executor. BusTub=
 only supports indexes with a single, unique integer column. There will <u>=
not</u> be duplicate keys in the test cases.</p>
<p><strong>Hint:</strong> Now that you have implemented all storage related=
 executors. In the following tasks, you can create tables and insert some v=
alues by yourself to test your own executor implementation! At this point, =
you should also have passed <a href=3D"https://15445.courses.cs.cmu.edu/fal=
l2022/project3/#testing">SQLLogicTests #1 to #5</a>.</p>
</div>
